// `Int / Int` yields `Float`, which is not a type this tier lowers: it supports
// `Int`/`Bool` and says so by name (issue #2201). Both sides used to lower the
// node to `i64.div_s` -- answering `3` where the language says `3.5`, a silent
// wrong value in the one position the tier DOES admit a `Float` (a `${...}`
// interpolation), and in the binding below.
//
// Both positions are here, because they reach different code. `ratio` is the
// scalar binding: the reference refuses it in `_bin_expr`, and it is the only
// document in the tree that reaches the port's `render_bin` refusal.
// `half` is the interpolation: the reference refuses it in the same place, and
// the port refuses the interpolation as `<<UNSUPPORTED-EXPR:interp>>` before it
// gets that far -- the port's function-only slice carries no interpolation.
//
// This is not a byte-agreement case, so it cannot live in the tier's CORPUS.
// The line-coverage gate drives the reference over it here, which is the only
// way
//
//   backends/wasm/emit.py::_refuse_true_division
//
// ever runs. The refusal's text is asserted by
// tests/test_selfhost_emit_wasm.py::
// test_a_true_division_the_reference_refuses_is_refused_by_name_here_too.
fn ratio(a: Int, b: Int) { return a / b }

fn half(a: Int, b: Int) -> Str { return `${a / b}` }
